    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "48b6c086_f90b1800",
        "filename": "chip/g/usb_spi_v2.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 626,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000039
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-10-01T02:34:39Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I guess this is confusing. The idea is that the read count needs to be limited in the first iteration, when there still is a write portion.\n\nTo make it clearer I change read_limit calculation to always subtract \u0027this_write_count\u0027 from the SPI buffer size, \u0027this_write_count\u0027 is set to zero after the first SPI subtransaction in the end of the first iteration, so the buffer size adjustment will happen only on the first iteration.\n\nlast_sub_transaction affects both reads and writes - this is a signal to the SPI driver to deassert CS after this transaction.\n\nlast_sub_transaction should be set in a separate check. Let me try to make the logic clearer.",
      "parentUuid": "e149f8b2_a3c9d94a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 626,
        "startChar": 3,
        "endLine": 626,
        "endChar": 55
      },
      "revId": "0dc5e657759e133fc521617135f8c4c41b35280b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "ead65bda_ca88edce",
        "filename": "chip/g/usb_spi_v2.c",
        "patchSetId": 3
      },
      "lineNbr": 644,
      "author": {
        "id": 1152319
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-09-30T23:25:59Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "what if total_read_count \u003d\u003d 0 and we didn\u0027t allocate the buffer?\n\noverall, is it possible to extract the code that deals with sending the buffer into a sub-function? and make sure that all buffer allocation/deallocation is done outside?\n\nI also feel it will be just easier to do \n```\nif (total_read_count) {\n  return process_write_read_request();\n} else {\n  return process_pure_write_request();\n}\n```\nat the top level and have two separate functions even if the code is duplicated (and that part can be extracted into a common function) - the logic will be clearer than following individual nested \u0027if\u0027s",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 644,
        "startChar": 4,
        "endLine": 644,
        "endChar": 22
      },
      "revId": "0dc5e657759e133fc521617135f8c4c41b35280b",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
